# Efficient Simulation of Quantum Systems by Quantum Computers

Christof Zalka\thanks{Institut für theoretische Physik, Universität Bern, Switzerland. BUTP--96/11.}

March 25, 1996 (arXiv:quant-ph/9603026v2, 14 Aug 1996)

###### Abstract.

We show that the time evolution of the wave function of a quantum
mechanical many particle system can be implemented very efficiently on a
quantum computer. The computational cost is comparable to the cost of a
conventional simulation of the corresponding classical system. We sketch how
results of interest (e.g. energy spectra) can be obtained, and indicate that
ultimately the simulation of quantum field theory might be possible on large
quantum computers.

## 1 Quantum Computers (QCs)

Quantum computers are still imaginary devices. A \(l\)-bit QC consists of
\(l\) qubits acted on by exterior fields inducing few-qubit unitaries.
Shor (1994) gave a polynomial-time factoring algorithm. The main
technical hurdle is decoherence; we assume an ideal QC.

## 2 Simulating Quantum Systems

I present an actual implementation of the simulation of a quantum
mechanical many-particle system on a general-purpose QC.

### 2.1 Quantum mechanical particle in 1 dimension

Discretize with periodic BC:
\begin{equation}
a_n = \psi(n\Delta x),\qquad a_{n+N} = a_n. \label{eq:1}
\end{equation}
Encode
\begin{equation}
|\psi\rangle = \sum_{n=0}^{N-1} a_n\,|n\rangle,\qquad N = 2^l. \label{eq:2}
\end{equation}
Short-time Green's function
\begin{equation}
G(x_1,x_2,\Delta t) = k\,e^{i\frac{m(x_1-x_2)^2}{2\Delta t} - iV(x_1)\Delta t}. \label{eq:3}
\end{equation}
Acting on amplitudes:
\begin{equation}
|n\rangle \to \frac{1}{\sqrt{N}} \sum_{n'} e^{-i\frac{m(n-n')^2\Delta x^2}{2\Delta t} + iV(n\Delta x)\Delta t}\,|n'\rangle. \label{eq:4}
\end{equation}
This factors as **diagonal · Fourier · diagonal**:
\begin{equation}
|n\rangle \to \frac{1}{\sqrt{N}}\,e^{-i\frac{m n^2\Delta x^2}{2\Delta t} + iV(n\Delta x)\Delta t}\,\sum_{n'} e^{i m n n'\Delta x^2/\Delta t}\,e^{-i\frac{m n'^2\Delta x^2}{2\Delta t}}\,|n'\rangle. \label{eq:5}
\end{equation}
With \(m\Delta x^2/\Delta t = A\cdot 2\pi/N\) (\(A\in\mathbb Z\)), the phase
is periodic and the QFT applies. The QFT/FFT on a QC costs
\(\sim l^2/2\) local gates.

Diagonal unitaries \(|n\rangle\to e^{icF(n)}|n\rangle\) are realized via an
ancilla register:
\begin{equation}
|n,0\rangle \to |n,F(n)\rangle \to e^{icF(n)}|n,F(n)\rangle \to |n,0\rangle. \label{eq:8}
\end{equation}
The special case \(F(n)=n\) costs only \(l\) single-qubit phases.

### 2.2 Many particles and field theory

For \(n\) particles in 3D we need \(3n\) registers. Multi-particle diagonal
couplings
\begin{equation}
|n,n',n''\rangle \to e^{i c F(n,n',n'')}|n,n',n''\rangle \label{eq:10}
\end{equation}
are handled analogously to (eq:8). Bosonic quantum field theory is
one-particle-per-site. Lattice QCD would need thousands of qubits.

### 2.3 Fermionic field theories

Use a Fock-space / occupation-number encoding (one qubit per fermion mode)
in place of Grassmann-valued functionals.

## 3 Other manipulations

### 3.1 Simulating a decay to obtain the ground state

Couple the system to an auxiliary energy-drain of 2-level systems with gaps
\(\Delta E = E_0 2^{-n}\), \(n=0,\dots,l\), and periodically reset the
drain; on average the system energy decreases toward the ground manifold.

### 3.2 Putting a wave function on a quantum computer

Prepare
\begin{equation}
|0\rangle \to k \sum_{n=0}^{2^l-1} \psi\!\left(n\frac{L}{2^l}\right)|n\rangle,\qquad \psi(x+L)=\psi(x), \label{eq:11}
\end{equation}
by \(l\) levels of controlled O(2) rotations sized to the integrals
\begin{equation}
I_{i,k} = \int_{kL/2^i}^{(k+1)L/2^i} |\phi(x)|^2\,dx. \label{eq:12}
\end{equation}
First split:
\begin{equation}
|0,\dots\rangle \to \sqrt{I_{1,0}}|0,\dots\rangle + \sqrt{I_{1,1}}|1,\dots\rangle. \label{eq:13}
\end{equation}

## 4 Obtaining results of interest

### 4.1 Positions / field strengths

Direct measurement of a register in the computational basis yields sample
values; repeated runs give statistical estimates of \(n\)-point functions
and correlation lengths.

### 4.2 Arbitrary observables (von Neumann first-stage)

Couple the system to a 1D meter particle with \(\hat H = k\hat P\hat A\), so
\begin{equation}
\hat U(t)|\Psi_a\rangle|x\rangle = |\Psi_a\rangle |x + k a t\rangle,\qquad \hat A|\Psi_a\rangle = a|\Psi_a\rangle. \label{eq:17}
\end{equation}
Measuring the meter register yields an eigenvalue of \(\hat A\); repetition
gives the spectrum and weights, projecting the system into eigenstates.

## 5 Conclusion

Large ideal QCs could compute quantum-theoretic quantities out of reach of
classical machines, especially for strongly interacting field theories such
as QCD. Realization is contingent on suppressing decoherence and achieving
sufficient unitary precision.

